---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import TopPick from "../components/TopPick.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Home Generators for Power Outages",
    "url": "/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Gasoline Generators for Home Backup",
    "url": "/best-gas-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Small Generators for Home Use",
    "url": "/best-small-generator-for-home/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Portable Power Stations Ranked",
    "url": "/best-portable-power-station/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-02", modified: "2026-11-02" });
const title = "Generac Generator Cost: What a Standby System Really Runs";
const description =
  "What a Generac standby generator costs installed, line by line: unit prices by size, transfer switch, gas and electrical work, permits and ten years of upkeep.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Generac Generator Cost",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/generac-generator-cost/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a Generac 22kW cost installed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most straightforward 22kW installs on existing natural gas land roughly between $10,000 and $16,000, with the generator unit around $5,500 to $6,500 of that. Long gas or electrical runs, a propane tank, panel upgrades, load management modules and difficult sites push totals higher. Get at least three itemized quotes, because prices for the same job vary widely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does Generac installation cost so much?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A standby install involves several trades: an electrician for the transfer switch and wiring, a gas fitter for piping and sometimes a meter upgrade, site preparation for the pad, and permits and inspections for each. The labor, materials and coordination often equal or exceed the cost of the generator itself, especially when the unit is far from the meter and panel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size Generac do I need for my house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on your loads, not just square footage. Homes with gas heat and appliances often do well with 14 to 18kW for essentials or 22kW for nearly everything. All-electric homes and homes with multiple air conditioners may need 24kW or more, or load management. Ask the dealer for a load calculation based on your appliances."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to run a Generac during an outage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on load and fuel. A 22kW at around half load uses roughly 2 gallons of propane an hour, which can cost over $100 a day of continuous running at typical propane prices. Natural gas is usually much cheaper per day. Weekly exercise cycles use only a few dollars of fuel a month."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much is annual Generac maintenance?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Dealer service visits typically cost around $200 to $400 a year and include oil, filters, spark plugs and a battery and system check. Many dealers sell annual plans in that range. Add a starting battery every three to five years, roughly $50 to $150. After a long outage, an extra oil change may be needed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a Generac generator qualify for a tax credit?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fuel-burning generators generally do not qualify for federal clean-energy tax credits. Some states, utilities, municipalities or insurers offer rebates, discounts or medical-need programs for backup power, so check locally. A medical necessity may also affect tax treatment in some cases; ask a tax professional rather than relying on a sales pitch."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a Generac myself to save money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not legally in most places, and not safely. Standby installs involve service-entrance electrical work, gas piping and permits with inspections, and Generac's warranty typically requires installation by qualified professionals. A DIY site prep, like clearing a path or leveling the area, may save a little. The electrical and gas connections should be done by licensed trades."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Power & Generators",
    tags: [
      "generac generator cost",
      "generac installation cost",
      "whole house generator cost",
      "generac 22kw price",
      "standby generator maintenance cost",
      "generac guardian price"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Outage Generator Picks",
    link: "/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Cost Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Research-Based
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Generac Generator Cost: <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">The Full Bill</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          The generator is usually less than half the invoice. Here is where the rest of a typical Generac quote goes, and which line items you can actually shrink.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#price-tiers"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See the Cost Breakdown
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#price-tiers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Price Tiers at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#unit-prices" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Unit Prices by Size</a>
          <a href="#install-items" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Installation Line Items</a>
          <a href="#what-moves-price" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. What Pushes Quotes Up</a>
          <a href="#right-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Sizing Without Overpaying</a>
          <a href="#ownership" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Fuel and Upkeep Costs</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cheaper Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#lower-quote" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Lowering Your Quote</a>
          <a href="#worth-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Is It Worth It?</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A whole-home Generac standby system usually costs somewhere between $10,000 and $20,000 installed, and the generator itself is often only a third to a half of that. The rest is a transfer switch, an electrician, a gas fitter, a pad, permits and sometimes a propane tank or a bigger gas meter. Knowing those pieces is how you read a quote and how you lower one.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Generac Price Tiers at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generac sells three very different things under one name. Its portable generators are retail products you can buy off a shelf. Its air-cooled Guardian standby units are the home-backup mainstay, installed permanently outside and wired to an automatic transfer switch. Its liquid-cooled Protector line and PWRcell battery systems sit at the high end. The price ranges below are typical installed totals at the time of writing; local labor rates and site conditions move them a lot.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">System</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical output</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical installed total</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Usually covers</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Portable plus transfer switch or interlock</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-8kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500-$3,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Selected essential circuits, manual start</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 10-14kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-14kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$7,000-$11,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials plus some comfort loads, maybe one AC</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 18-22kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18-22kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$10,000-$16,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most homes, including a central AC with load management</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 24-26kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-26kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$12,000-$19,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Larger homes, two AC systems with load management</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protector liquid-cooled</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30-60kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$20,000-$40,000+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very large or all-electric homes, light commercial</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most homeowners end up in the 18 to 22kW range, and quotes commonly reported by homeowners and dealers cluster somewhere around $12,000 to $15,000 for a straightforward 22kW install on existing natural gas. That figure climbs quickly with long gas runs, a propane tank set from scratch, a panel or meter upgrade, or a difficult site.
          </p>

          <p>
            The short version: budget for the unit, then roughly the same amount again for installation, and then some annual upkeep. If a quote looks dramatically below that pattern, find out what it leaves out. If it is dramatically above, ask for an itemized version.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical 22kW Install, Rough Shares</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">40-50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Generator unit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10-15%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Automatic transfer switch</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25-35%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Electrical and gas labor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5-10%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pad, permits, startup, misc</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="unit-prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Generac Unit Prices by Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generac sells standby units through authorized dealers, and many dealers quote installed prices only, so unit-only prices are best treated as a guide. The figures below are typical ranges for the generator alone at the time of writing; regional variation and promotions can move them by 10 to 15 percent or more.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Model</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rated output (LP / NG)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical unit price</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 10kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10kW / 9kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3,200-$4,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 14kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14kW / 14kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,200-$5,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 18kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18kW / 17kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,900-$5,700</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 22kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22kW / 19.5kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5,500-$6,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Guardian 24-26kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 26kW on LP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,500-$7,800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protector QS liquid-cooled</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30-60kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$13,000-$25,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">GP8000E portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8,000W running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,000-$1,300</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice that output differs by fuel. Air-cooled standby engines make less power on natural gas than on propane, so a 22kW unit is really about a 19.5kW unit on a natural gas line. If you size your house to the edge of a model's propane rating and then run it on natural gas, you have less margin than you thought.
          </p>

          <p>
            The price curve is not linear. The steps from 14 to 18 to 22kW are relatively small in dollar terms because those units share much of their design, which is why 22kW is such a popular choice. Jumping to the largest air-cooled sizes, and especially to liquid-cooled Protector units, costs disproportionately more for the extra output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generac's transfer switch is sometimes bundled with the generator and sometimes sold separately. A 200-amp whole-house automatic transfer switch commonly adds around $600 to $1,500 for the hardware alone, more for service-rated or smart-management versions. Make sure any unit price you compare includes or excludes the switch consistently.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install-items" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Installation: Every Line Item on a Typical Quote
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation is where the real variation lives, and where most homeowners are surprised. A standby generator is a small construction project touching electrical, gas and sometimes structural work, with permits and inspections for each. A well-run dealer coordinates all of it; you pay for every trade involved.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Line item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electrical labor and materials</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500-$4,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">ATS install, wiring from generator to switch and panel</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas piping (natural gas)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$500-$3,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rises with distance from meter and pipe size needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas meter or regulator upgrade</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0-$1,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Utility may need a larger meter for the extra load</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Propane tank set (if no NG)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500-$4,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buried or above-ground 250-1,000 gallon tank plus lines</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pad and site prep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$200-$1,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Composite pad often included; gravel base or concrete extra</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Permits and inspections</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$100-$1,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrical, gas, sometimes zoning; varies widely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Startup, registration and walkthrough</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0-$500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often bundled; confirms warranty activation</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The electrical work includes mounting the automatic transfer switch, usually next to the meter or main panel, and running conductors from the generator to the switch. If your main panel is old, full or not compatible with a service-rated switch, the electrician may recommend replacing or upgrading it, which can add $1,500 to $4,000 on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas work depends on distance and capacity. A 22kW unit needs a lot of gas flow when it starts and runs, and your existing gas line may be too small to feed it alongside a furnace and water heater. That can mean a larger pipe run from the meter, and sometimes a call to the utility for a bigger meter. Homes without natural gas need a propane tank sized for days of runtime, which is a significant extra cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement rules affect cost too. Generac's air-cooled units are commonly allowed as close as 18 inches from the house when local code permits, with about 5 feet of clearance from windows, doors, vents and other openings, but local codes, utility rules and HOA requirements can push the unit farther away, which lengthens both electrical and gas runs. Ask your installer early where the unit can legally go.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">READING A QUOTE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ask for four separate numbers.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Unit and transfer switch, electrical labor and materials, gas work, and permits and site prep. With those four lines you can compare quotes fairly, see where a high bid is padding, and spot what a low bid left out.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-moves-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Pushes a Generac Quote Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Distance.</strong> Every foot between the gas meter, the generator and the electrical panel adds pipe, wire, trenching and labor. A unit placed beside the meter and panel on the same wall is the cheapest install. A unit on the opposite side of the house, or across a driveway, can add thousands.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fuel.</strong> Homes with natural gas already at the street have a big cost advantage. Without it, a propane tank, its pad or burial, regulators and lines add a large chunk, and you may lease or buy the tank depending on your propane supplier. Underground tanks cost more than above-ground ones but are often preferred visually and by HOAs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Panel condition.</strong> Older panels, panels from brands with known problems, full panels and homes with 100-amp service may need work before a transfer switch can be added. Some installs add a smart management module to shed large loads like air conditioning or an electric dryer when the generator is near capacity; each module typically adds a few hundred dollars installed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Region and season.</strong> Labor costs differ by a factor of two between some markets, and demand spikes after major storms. Quotes in the weeks after a hurricane or ice storm are often higher and lead times longer. Permit fees and requirements also vary widely; some jurisdictions require zoning review, noise compliance or engineered drawings.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Site.</strong> Slopes, rock, tree roots, tight side yards that require hand-carrying a 400-pound unit, or needing a crane or extra crew to get the generator over a fence all show up on the invoice. A good dealer will do a site visit before quoting; be wary of any quote given sight unseen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="right-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing a Generac Without Overpaying
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dealers often recommend sizing for the whole house. That is reasonable for many homes, but square footage alone is a poor guide. What matters is your largest loads, how many air conditioning systems you have, whether your heat, water heater, range and dryer are gas or electric, and whether you have a well pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Essentials coverage: 10 to 14kW</h3>

          <p>
            A smaller Guardian with a transfer switch that covers selected circuits handles the refrigerator, freezer, furnace or boiler, sump pump, well pump, lights, internet and kitchen outlets, and often a single smaller air conditioner. For homes with gas heat and gas appliances, this can save $2,000 to $4,000 over a whole-house unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-house coverage: 18 to 26kW</h3>

          <p>
            The 22kW is the most common whole-house choice for typical homes with one or two central air systems. Pairing it with Generac's load-management modules lets it keep the air conditioning running while preventing an electric dryer or second compressor from overloading the generator. That combination often lets a 22kW unit do what would otherwise need something larger.
          </p>

          <p>
            All-electric homes are where sizing gets expensive. Electric heat strips, heat pump backup heat, an electric tank water heater and an electric range can add up quickly, and a whole-house approach may point toward a liquid-cooled unit. Before accepting that, ask whether load management or switching a few heavy appliances off during outages would let an air-cooled unit do the job. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">outage generator guide</a> walks through running and starting watts in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask the dealer to perform an actual load calculation, ideally with your utility bills or a review of your appliance nameplates. A quote that names a size without asking what is in your house is guessing, and the guess is usually high.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel, Maintenance and Ten-Year Ownership Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying the generator is the big check, but standby units also cost money to own. Most of that is predictable: annual maintenance, a battery every few years, the small amount of fuel used by weekly exercise, and the fuel burned during outages.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Maintenance.</strong> Air-cooled Guardian units need oil, oil filter, air filter and spark plugs on a schedule set by run hours and time, typically at least once a year. Dealer service visits commonly run $200 to $400, and many dealers sell annual plans in a similar range. Skipping service risks a no-start when it matters and can complicate warranty claims.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery.</strong> The starting battery typically lasts three to five years and costs roughly $50 to $150 to replace, more if installed during a service call. <strong>Exercise fuel.</strong> The weekly self-test runs for a few minutes at low speed and uses only a small amount of gas, usually a few dollars a month or less.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Outage fuel.</strong> This is the variable. A 22kW unit at around half load uses roughly 2 gallons of propane an hour, per typical Generac fuel consumption charts, or a couple of hundred cubic feet of natural gas an hour. At recent propane prices that can mean well over $100 a day of continuous running; natural gas is usually much cheaper per day. Generators run at lighter loads most of the time, so real costs are often lower.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
